+# FlyWeb: release after 3.2.2 (softmac `seguridad/siguiente-3`), V8 fix a2c4c5c9dd (crbug 590253989)
+
+SEGURIDAD-PORTES-33, 27-11-2026 (softmac `FlyWeb/seguridad/siguiente-3/brave-core/0013`). V8 15.6 a2c4c5c9dd
+(15.6.75.90, 27-11; bugs 590971898 and 590253989; cherry-pick of main 8a6bd48d91; M159 twin b10dd63a16, 15.9.128.44).
+Applied as is to 15.6.75.66 (`maglev-graph-builder.cc` and `maglev-ir.h` already had FlyWeb patches; no conflict;
+every file's base blob matches upstream's). Severity not published.
+
+- In Maglev and Turbolev, the enum cache load of `for-in` (`VisitForInNext`) did not keep the enumerator map alive. If
+  that map was collected, the GC could right-trim the enum cache, and the following accesses read out of bounds.
+  TurboFan already had the fix (crrev.com/c/8820107).
+- Now a new Maglev node, `Retain`, keeps the enumerator map alive across the enum cache load (Turbolev lowers it to
+  Turboshaft's `Retain`; a constant map is retained on the code object). New tests
+  `mjsunit/maglev/regress-590253989.js` and `mjsunit/turbolev/regress-590253989.js`.
+
+**Cache epoch:** covered by the single 40 -> 41 bump of `siguiente-3` (`brave-core/0001`); no bytecode change.
+
+To check (D8-LINUX): build and run mjsunit `maglev/regress-590253989` and `turbolev/regress-590253989` (with their
+flags), `maglev/*` and `turbolev/*`, with and without `--maglev`.
+
